        salesDrivers: ["**Winning back Heubach's lost customers**: the combined business sold about €1bn a year before insolvency, far above today's level.", "**Cutting costs in Europe**: moving commodity lines from Frankfurt to Roha in India, merging four SAP systems and building a Pune service centre; a Works Council restructuring cost is still to come.", "**Market growth of only 3–4% a year**: pigment demand grows with the world economy, and US and European paint demand is subdued.", "**Guidance**: FY27 revenue ₹9,800–10,200cr and EBITDA about ₹800cr, rising to ₹12,000–14,000cr and ₹1,400–1,500cr by FY29, kept rather than raised after a strong Q1.", "**Little capex needed**: management says no new volume capacity is required to FY29; FY26 capex was ₹270cr and CRISIL expects ₹150–250cr a year."],
        inputs: "**Organic chemical intermediates** (largely oil-derived aromatics) and **inorganic raw materials** for the pigments, plus energy, which is 6–7% of revenue and was hit by the 2026 Middle East shock. Materials were 51% of sales in FY26.",
        costEffect: "**Passed on, with a lag.** Prices are negotiated quarterly and recent rises only passed on higher costs ‘to build trust’. Materials are a moderate share of costs; staff (17% of sales, much of it in Europe) and energy matter as much. **Rule of thumb:** a 10% rise in material costs not passed on would cut the margin by about 5 points (6% to 1%). **What happened:** there is no Indian price index for its mix of inputs, and costs were not what moved the margin. It fell from 13% in FY24 to 11% in FY25 and 6% in FY26 because the acquired group, now most of sales, earned only 3–6%. Quarterly, it dropped from 8% to 2% in Q3 FY26 (acquisition accounting) and recovered to 10% in Q1 FY27 as the acquired group reached 6.5%.",
        competitors: [{ name: 'DIC / Sun Chemical', note: 'Japanese-owned world leader in pigments, the only group larger than Sudarshan after the Heubach deal.' }, { name: 'Other Western pigment makers', note: 'Have under 1% of their assets in Asia, so higher costs than Sudarshan.' }, { name: 'Chinese and Indian pigment makers', note: 'Compete hard in commodity colours on price; weaker in high-performance and car grades.' }],
        position: "**World no. 2 in pigments**, with the broadest range (about 1,600 products) and the lowest-cost footprint among the global leaders.",
